<p><strong>To track actual cortisol, you need the right hormone test and a plan for reading the result.</strong></p>
<p>To track everyday stress and recovery, write down your sleep, how stressed you feel and any wearable readings. These are two different things. A journal or an Apple Watch app can't tell you how much cortisol you have.</p>
<p>The Cortisol+ team publishes this guide. We make a wearable wellness app, not a cortisol test. This is general information, not personal testing advice.</p>

<h2>How can I better track my cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>Start by deciding what you want to learn.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Got a medical worry or an abnormal test result?</strong> Ask your doctor which test, collection time and follow-up make sense.</li>
  <li><strong>Want to understand your daily routine?</strong> Use the same observation log every day.</li>
</ul>
<p>Keep lab results separate from app scores. Don't read a change in sleep or heart rate variability (HRV) as proof that a hormone changed.</p>
<div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>"What is my cortisol level?"</p>
    <p class={cardText}>The right blood, saliva or urine test.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}><strong>Can't tell you:</strong> the cause of a result without medical context.</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>"What changes on stressful days?"</p>
    <p class={cardText}>A dated journal of sleep, routine and how stressed you feel.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}><strong>Can't tell you:</strong> your cortisol. A journal doesn't measure it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class={card}>
    <p class={cardTitle}>"How do my wearable readings vary?"</p>
    <p class={cardText}>Compare the same device and measure over similar periods.</p>
    <p class={cardMuted}><strong>Can't tell you:</strong> hormone levels. HRV and wellness scores aren't hormone levels.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p><a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">MedlinePlus explains the types of cortisol tests</a> and why collection timing matters. Our <a href="/cortisol/test/#collection-record">at-home cortisol collection checklist</a> lists the questions to ask before you order or collect a sample.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_tour_now.webp">
  Keep <strong>two separate notes</strong>: one for lab results, one for app scores. It stops you mixing them up.
</ZenTip>

<h2 id="cortisol-tracking-app">What does a cortisol tracking app actually track?</h2>
<p><strong>A cortisol tracking app may store hormone-test results, or it may read wearable stress signals. Check which one it does.</strong></p>
<p>Cortisol+ is an iPhone wellness app. It uses the Apple Health data you allow to help you look at HRV, sleep and recovery patterns. Its score is not a blood, saliva or urine cortisol measurement.</p>
<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-[color:var(--color-ink-muted)]">Choose a tracking approach by the question you want answered</p>
<div class="not-prose my-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
