<h2 id="iphone-app">Which cortisol tracking app should I choose for iPhone?</h2>
<p><strong>Choose by your goal.</strong> If you want to explore Apple Health readings with an explained wellness score, consider Cortisol+. If you need an actual cortisol result, an HRV app can't give you that.</p>
<p>Look at the <a href="/how-it-works/">Cortisol+ methodology</a> and <a href="/stress/apple-watch/#walkthrough">app walkthrough</a> before you decide if it fits your routine.</p>
<p>Cortisol+ is free to download on the App Store. Analysis needs a subscription or an eligible trial. Check compatibility and the current offer before you subscribe.</p>

<h2 id="free-options">Is there a free cortisol tracking app?</h2>
<p><strong>For a free start, look at your wearable readings in Apple Health and keep an observation journal.</strong> Neither one measures cortisol.</p>
<p>"Free to download" doesn't mean every analysis feature is free. Cortisol+ needs a subscription or eligible trial for analysis. Compare the ongoing price, trial eligibility and features shown in each app.</p>

<h2>A practical daily tracking routine</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Write down the date and when you slept.</strong> Note bedtime, wake time and any wake-ups in your own words.</li>
  <li><strong>Add how you felt.</strong> Use the same words or scale each day for stress and energy. This is your own report, not a diagnosis.</li>
  <li><strong>Note what was different.</strong> Changes in exercise, caffeine timing, alcohol, illness or routine. Don't change medicines to improve a graph.</li>
  <li><strong>Check your wearable readings.</strong> Confirm the source, units and times. Missing data is not a low reading.</li>
  <li><strong>Look for patterns without naming a cause.</strong> "These changed together" is worth looking into. It is not proof that cortisol changed.</li>
</ol>
<p>Use the <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free seven-day stress and sleep journal</a> to get started. Seven days is just a handy length, not a tested cortisol-testing schedule.</p>

<h2>What should I record alongside a cortisol test?</h2>
<p><strong>Keep the lab report, units, reference range, sample type and collection date and time together.</strong></p>
<p>Tell the doctor who ordered the test about your medicines and any changes in your routine. Follow the collection instructions you were given, not a general schedule from online.</p>
<p>Don't compare a saliva result directly with a blood result. And don't assume different labs use the same ranges.</p>

<h2 id="separate-records">How do I compare my app score with a cortisol test?</h2>
<p><strong>Keep them in separate records. Don't convert one into the other.</strong></p>
<p>A cortisol result belongs with its sample type, collection time, units and lab reference range. A wearable score belongs with its app, the readings behind it and the time window. Our advice is to look at the context together, without expecting the numbers to match.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Lab record:</strong> keep the original report and ask the doctor to explain it. <a href="https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/">MedlinePlus describes the different sample types and how to prepare</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Wearable record:</strong> note which readings were used and whether any were missing. The <a href="/how-it-works/">Cortisol+ methodology</a> explains our model, which is not a hormone test.</li>
  <li><strong>Your own notes:</strong> write down how you felt separately. <a href="https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/stress">NCCIH separates research on self-reported stress from body measurements</a>. Feeling calmer doesn't give you a cortisol level.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, a better app score after a restful night tells you about that model's inputs. It doesn't show that a saliva result would be lower. This is advice on organizing information, not a result from a clinical comparison of Cortisol+ and testing.</p>

<h2>Can I track cortisol without a test?</h2>
<p><strong>You can track stress-related notes without a test, but you can't confirm your cortisol level from them.</strong></p>
<p><a href="/stress/apple-watch/">Apple Watch records signals such as heart rate, HRV and sleep</a>. It does not measure cortisol directly. An app can interpret those inputs, but that interpretation needs its own evidence and limits.</p>

<h2>Where does Cortisol+ fit?</h2>
<p><strong>Cortisol+ helps you look at your wearable signals and the factors behind its wellness score.</strong> Read the <a href="/how-it-works/">methodology</a>, check the records behind the score, and compare similar periods.</p>
<p>Its clinical accuracy against lab cortisol testing has not been shown. The app can't decide whether you need testing, or confirm that a habit lowered your cortisol.</p>
